    fullText: 'Narmada Bachao Andolan v. Union of India addressed the conflict between development and environmental protection in the context of the Sardar Sarovar Dam project on the Narmada river. The NBA, led by Medha Patkar, challenged the project arguing that it would displace over 41,000 families (predominantly tribal) and cause irreversible environmental damage without adequate rehabilitation. The project aimed to provide irrigation and drinking water to millions in drought-prone Gujarat, Rajasthan, and Maharashtra. The three-judge bench delivered a split verdict. Justices Kirpal and Anand (majority) held that large dams are essential for development and the benefits to millions of people outweigh the displacement of thousands. The court held that displacement caused by development projects is constitutional as long as proper rehabilitation is provided. The majority directed that construction could continue but rehabilitation must be completed pari passu with dam construction—before submergence at each stage. Justice Bharucha dissented, arguing that the environmental clearance was given without proper assessment and the rehabilitation measures were inadequate. The judgment established the principle that displacement for development is permissible but the state has an absolute obligation to rehabilitate affected persons. The case became a touchstone in the debate between development and environmental justice.',
