    fullText: 'Vellore Citizens Welfare Forum v. Union of India is a landmark environmental law judgment that incorporated international environmental law principles into Indian jurisprudence. The case was filed against untreated effluent discharge by tanneries and other industries in the Vellore district of Tamil Nadu into the Palar river, which was the primary source of drinking water for the surrounding population. The pollution had rendered the river water unusable and contaminated groundwater. Justice Kuldip Singh held that the Precautionary Principle and the Polluter Pays Principle are part of the environmental law of India. The Precautionary Principle requires that when there are threats of serious or irreversible environmental damage, lack of full scientific certainty shall not be used as a reason for postponing measures to prevent environmental degradation. The state and statutory authorities must anticipate, prevent, and attack the causes of environmental degradation. The Polluter Pays Principle means that the absolute liability of the polluting industry extends not only to compensation of victims but also to the cost of restoring environmental degradation. The polluter is liable to pay for individual damage as well as the cost of reversing the degraded environment. The court directed the Central Government to constitute an authority under the Environment Protection Act to deal with the situation, and imposed a fine of Rs 10,000 on each tannery as interim pollution compensation.',
