    fullText: 'I.R. Coelho v. State of Tamil Nadu is a landmark nine-judge bench decision that strengthened the basic structure doctrine in relation to the Ninth Schedule. The Ninth Schedule was added to the Constitution by the First Amendment in 1951 to protect land reform laws from judicial review. Article 31B provided that laws placed in the Ninth Schedule could not be challenged as violating fundamental rights. Over time, legislatures placed numerous laws (not just land reform) in the Ninth Schedule to immunize them from judicial scrutiny. By 2007, over 280 laws had been placed in the Schedule. The question before the court was whether such immunity was absolute or subject to basic structure review. The nine-judge bench, led by Chief Justice Y.K. Sabharwal, unanimously held that laws placed in the Ninth Schedule after April 24, 1973 (the date of the Kesavananda Bharati judgment) are open to challenge if they violate fundamental rights that form part of the basic structure. The court reasoned that if constitutional amendments themselves are subject to basic structure review, then a fortiori, laws that are protected by constitutional amendments (through the Ninth Schedule) must also be subject to such review. The court held that the fundamental right to equality (Article 14), freedoms under Article 19, and right to life and liberty under Article 21 form part of the basic structure. Any Ninth Schedule law that abrogates these rights can be struck down. This effectively ended Parliament\'s ability to place laws beyond judicial review.',
