    fullText: 'Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras was among the first cases to test the scope of fundamental rights under the newly adopted Constitution. Romesh Thappar was the editor and publisher of "Cross Roads," a weekly journal printed in Bombay. The Madras government, under the Madras Maintenance of Public Order Act 1949, banned the entry and circulation of the journal in the state, claiming it was necessary for public safety and maintenance of public order. Thappar challenged the ban under Article 19(1)(a) which guarantees freedom of speech and expression. The Supreme Court, through Justice Patanjali Sastri, held that freedom of the press derives from the freedom of speech and expression guaranteed to citizens under Article 19(1)(a). The court struck down the ban, holding that the restriction was not saved by Article 19(2) which at that time only permitted restrictions on grounds of security of the state, friendly relations with foreign states, public order, decency or morality, or contempt of court. The court drew a distinction between "security of the state" and "public order," holding that a mere threat to public order, without endangering the security of the state itself, could not justify a complete ban on a publication. This decision, along with Brij Bhushan v. State of Delhi (1950), prompted the government to enact the First Constitutional Amendment in 1951, adding "public order" as an explicit ground for restricting free speech under Article 19(2).',
